      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Use trial and error to compute the following residue.\n\n${3}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,8=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $8,$ denoted ${3}^{-1},$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n3\\cdot x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8).\n$$\n\nNote that since $\\text{gcd}(3,8)=1,$ an inverse element exists and is unique up to congruence modulo $8.$\n\nSince the modulus $n=8$ is small, we can find the inverse $x$ by computing exponents of $3$ (modulo $8$) until we get a result of $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2} & ≡3\\cdot 3≡9≡1\\cdot 8+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $3\\cdot 3≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8).$ Therefore, the multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $8$ is $3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
