    "q-213409": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213409",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Use trial and error to compute the following residue.\n\n${7}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,12=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The multiplicative inverse of $7$ modulo $12,$ denoted ${7}^{-1},$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n7\\cdot x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12).\n$$\n\nNote that since $\\text{gcd}(7,12)=1,$ an inverse element exists and is unique up to congruence modulo $12.$\n\nSince the modulus $n=12$ is small, we can find the inverse $x$ by computing exponents of $7$ (modulo $12$) until we get a result of $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{2} & ≡7\\cdot 7≡49≡4\\cdot 12+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $7\\cdot 7≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12).$ Therefore, the multiplicative inverse of $7$ modulo $12$ is $7.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
