    "q-213511": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213511",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Fill in the blanks to make the following reasoning true.\n\nSince $\\text{gcd}(85,211)=$\n{{field-1}}, \nby the extended Euclidean algorithm, we have \n\n| $1\\,=\\,85\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+\\,211\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-3}}\n$).$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, ${85}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,211=$\n{{field-4}} (expressed as the smallest nonnegative residue).",
      "worked_solution": "First of all, notice that $\\text{gcd}(85,211)=1.$ So, the multiplicative inverse of $85$ modulo $211$ exists.\n\nThe multiplicative inverse of $85$ modulo $211$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n85\\cdot x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,211).\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $x,$ we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}211 & = & 85\\cdot 2 & + & 41 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 85 & = & 41\\cdot 2 & + & 3 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 41 & = & 3\\cdot 13 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 3 & = & 2\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}41 & =211-85\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & =85-41\\cdot 2 \\\\ 2 & =41-3\\cdot 13 \\\\ 1 & =3-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =3-2 \\\\  & =3-(41-3\\cdot 13) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 14-41 \\\\  & =(85-41\\cdot 2)\\cdot 14-41 \\\\  & =85\\cdot 14-41\\cdot 29 \\\\  & =85\\cdot 14-(211-85\\cdot 2)\\cdot 29 \\\\  & =85\\cdot 72-211\\cdot 29 \\\\  & =85\\cdot 72+211\\cdot (-29)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result modulo $211,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}85\\cdot 72+211\\cdot (-29) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,211) \\\\ 85\\cdot 72 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,211)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $85\\cdot 72≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,211),$ which means that ${85}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,211=72.$",
