    "q-213528": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213528",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Fill in the blanks to make the following reasoning true.\n\nSince $\\text{gcd}(69,82)=$\n{{field-1}}, \nby the extended Euclidean algorithm, we have \n\n| $1\\,=\\,69\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+\\,82\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-3}}\n$).$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, ${69}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,82=$\n{{field-4}} (expressed as the smallest nonnegative residue).",
      "worked_solution": "First of all, notice that $\\text{gcd}(69,82)=1.$ So, the multiplicative inverse of $69$ modulo $82$ exists.\n\nThe multiplicative inverse of $69$ modulo $82$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n69\\cdot x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,82).\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $x,$ we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}82 & = & 69\\cdot 1 & + & 13 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 69 & = & 13\\cdot 5 & + & 4 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 13 & = & 4\\cdot 3 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =82-69 \\\\ 4 & =69-13\\cdot 5 \\\\ 1 & =13-4\\cdot 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =13-4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =13-(69-13\\cdot 5)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =13-69\\cdot 3+13\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =13\\cdot 16-69\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =(82-69)\\cdot 16-69\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =82\\cdot 16-69\\cdot 16-69\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =82\\cdot 16-69\\cdot 19 \\\\  & =69\\cdot (-19)+82\\cdot 16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result modulo $82,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}69\\cdot (-19)+82\\cdot 16 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,82) \\\\ 69\\cdot (-19) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,82) \\\\ 69\\cdot (82-19) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,82) \\\\ 69\\cdot 63 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,82)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $69\\cdot 63≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,82),$ which means that ${69}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,82=63.$",
